(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-pharm-e2-master-drill"]={"title":"Master Drill","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic is soothing on an inflamed eye, so it may be used even when bacterial infection is not confirmed?","choices":["Erythromycin","Azithromycin","Ciprofloxacin","Tobramycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is soothing on the inflamed eye, and it is dirt cheap. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which class of eye drop causes REBOUND HYPEREMIA if used for too long?","choices":["Olopatadine","Naphazoline","Cromolyn","Ketorolac"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Naphazoline is a topical vasoconstrictor, and prolonged use leads to rebound hyperemia after discontinuation; nasal decongestant sprays share the risk. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which class is FIRST LINE for glaucoma and the most commonly used?","choices":["Timolol","Dorzolamide","Latanoprost","Pilocarpine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The prostaglandin analogs, which increase aqueous outflow. Beta blockers are second line. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which drug causes a DRY COUGH from bradykinin accumulating in the lungs?","choices":["Losartan","Amlodipine","Metoprolol","Lisinopril"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors stop bradykinin breakdown; the cough is not dose- or agent-related. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which statin has MINIMAL CYP metabolism, so CYP3A4 inhibitors affect it least?","choices":["Rosuvastatin","Atorvastatin","Lovastatin","Simvastatin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rosuvastatin's major metabolic route is listed as minimal CYP, unlike the CYP3A4 statins. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic is used for prophylaxis of ophthalmia neonatorum?","choices":["Gatifloxacin","Erythromycin","Sulfacetamide","Bacitracin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The 0.5% ointment, for superficial conjunctival or corneal infection and for newborn prophylaxis. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A patient asks how long they may use a vasoconstrictor eye drop. What is the limit?","choices":["Less than 72 hours","Less than six weeks","Less than two weeks","No limit"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vasoconstrictor drops are for short-term use under two weeks; prolonged use leads to rebound hyperemia after they are stopped. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which glaucoma class changes EYELASH LENGTH and IRIS COLOR?","choices":["Brimonidine","Betaxolol","Brinzolamide","Bimatoprost"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The prostaglandin analogs, which is why one of them is also marketed for lash growth. They also cause conjunctival hyperemia. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A patient cannot tolerate an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor's cough. Which drug is the SWITCH?","choices":["Valsartan","Enalapril","Captopril","Ramipril"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin receptor blockers do not cause cough and have a lower incidence of angioedema, so patients may be switched to them. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which statin is a CYP3A4 SUBSTRATE, raised by verapamil and grapefruit juice?","choices":["Rosuvastatin","Lovastatin","Pravastatin","Pitavastatin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Atorvastatin, lovastatin and simvastatin are the CYP3A4 statins. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic is considerably more expensive and given less frequently, so it is not used as often clinically?","choices":["Erythromycin","Bacitracin","Azithromycin","Sulfacetamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Twice a day against four or more for erythromycin, but the cost keeps it off the shelf. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A patient using an over-the-counter vasoconstrictor drop has had no improvement. At what point should they stop and see a provider?","choices":["Two weeks","24 hours","Seven days","72 hours"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. No improvement within 72 hours means something more serious may be going on. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Why must a prostaglandin analog NOT be dosed more than once daily?","choices":["Exceeding once daily INHIBITS the pressure-lowering effect","It causes rebound hyperemia","It raises the risk of cataract","It causes bradycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. More is worse, not better, which is counterintuitive and therefore worth holding. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor has an INTRAVENOUS active form?","choices":["Lisinopril","Enalapril","Benazepril","Quinapril"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Enalapril is converted to enalaprilat, which is the intravenous form. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which statin is metabolized mainly by CYP2C9?","choices":["Simvastatin","Atorvastatin","Fluvastatin","Rosuvastatin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fluvastatin's major metabolic enzyme is CYP2C9, not CYP3A4. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic leaves a white precipitate in about one in six patients?","choices":["Moxifloxacin","Ofloxacin","Levofloxacin","Ciprofloxacin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A white precipitate in roughly 17%, which is specific to it among the fluoroquinolones. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Topical vasoconstrictors act on which receptor LOCALLY in the eye?","choices":["Alpha 1","Alpha 2","Beta 1","Beta 2"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Locally alpha 1, which constricts the conjunctival vessels. Systemically these imidazolines target alpha 2 instead, which is what makes an accidental ingestion dangerous. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which glaucoma agent is BETA 1 SELECTIVE?","choices":["Timolol","Betaxolol","Carteolol","Levobunolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The selective one, so it carries less risk of bronchoconstriction in a patient with asthma. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor has a SHORT half-life, the exception to once-daily dosing?","choices":["Lisinopril","Ramipril","Captopril","Trandolapril"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Most ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors are once daily; captopril's short half-life is the exception. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which statin is cleared by GLUCURONIDATION (UGT enzymes) rather than CYP?","choices":["Atorvastatin","Lovastatin","Fluvastatin","Pitavastatin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pitavastatin's major metabolic enzymes are UGT1A3 and UGT2B7. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic class must be avoided in a patient with a sulfonamide allergy?","choices":["Sulfacetamide","Bacitracin","Tobramycin","Trimethoprim with polymyxin B"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is a sulfonamide, so a sulfa allergy rules it out. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antihistamine is available over the counter?","choices":["Azelastine","Ketotifen","Bepotastine","Emedastine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The over-the-counter agent among the ophthalmic antihistamines. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Why is a NONSELECTIVE beta blocker more efficacious in the eye but worse tolerated?","choices":["There are more beta 1 receptors in the eye","It penetrates the cornea better","There are more beta 2 receptors in the eye","It has a longer half-life"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. So blocking both gives more effect, at the cost of beta 2 effects elsewhere. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which drug raises POTASSIUM, especially with renal disease or a potassium-sparing diuretic?","choices":["Nifedipine","Doxazosin","Clonidine","Candesartan"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin receptor blockers (like ACE inhibitors) cause hyperkalemia, most often with renal disease or potassium-sparing diuretics. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which statin can be used as HIGH-INTENSITY therapy?","choices":["Atorvastatin","Pravastatin","Fluvastatin","Lovastatin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Only atorvastatin and rosuvastatin reach high intensity, lowering LDL (low-density lipoprotein) by 50% or more. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by preventing transfer of mucopeptides into the growing wall?","choices":["Erythromycin","Bacitracin","Gentamicin","Ofloxacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A cell wall agent, which sets it apart from the protein-synthesis and DNA agents alongside it. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"H1 receptor blockers are not really antagonists. What are they?","choices":["Non-competitive antagonists","Partial agonists","Inverse agonists","Irreversible blockers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They cause histamine receptor INACTIVATION, and they remain competitive with histamine. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which glaucoma class risks worsening heart failure, bradycardia, heart block and increased airway resistance?","choices":["Latanoprost","Dorzolamide","Pilocarpine","Timolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Systemic beta blockade from a drop, which is why the nasolacrimal duct is worth occluding. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker treats PAROXYSMAL SUPRAVENTRICULAR TACHYCARDIA?","choices":["Verapamil","Amlodipine","Felodipine","Nisoldipine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Only the non-dihydropyridines slow atrioventricular conduction, so only they treat supraventricular tachycardia. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which is the OTHER statin, besides atorvastatin, that reaches HIGH INTENSITY?","choices":["Simvastatin","Rosuvastatin","Pitavastatin","Pravastatin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Atorvastatin and rosuvastatin are the only two high-intensity statins. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic class binds the 50S ribosome and blocks transpeptidation?","choices":["Tobramycin","Ciprofloxacin","Erythromycin","Sulfacetamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The macrolide mechanism: no protein synthesis, so the bacterium dies. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which class works within MINUTES and is typically preferred for ocular allergy?","choices":["Cromolyn","Lodoxamide","Nedocromil","Olopatadine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Antihistamines have an onset within minutes, are dosed once or twice daily, and are preferred over mast cell stabilizers. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which glaucoma class works by blocking beta receptors in the ciliary body epithelium to reduce aqueous production?","choices":["Levobunolol","Travoprost","Carbachol","Apraclonidine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Less catecholamine activation, less cyclic AMP, less aqueous made. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which dihydropyridine is approved for SUBARACHNOID HEMORRHAGE?","choices":["Nifedipine","Nimodipine","Nicardipine","Isradipine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nimodipine's approved indication is subarachnoid hemorrhage. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which drug is the CHOLESTEROL ABSORPTION INHIBITOR, acting at the intestinal brush border?","choices":["Colesevelam","Gemfibrozil","Ezetimibe","Evolocumab"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ezetimibe selectively inhibits intestinal cholesterol absorption, raising hepatic LDL (low-density lipoprotein) receptor expression. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic class binds the 30S subunit to interfere with protein synthesis?","choices":["Azithromycin","Bacitracin","Moxifloxacin","Gentamicin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The aminoglycoside mechanism. Its ocular risk is corneal ulceration and reactive keratoconjunctivitis after several days. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which class is NOT useful for acute allergy symptoms?","choices":["Cromolyn","Ketotifen","Naphazoline","Epinastine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mast cell stabilizers need 5 to 14 days for full efficacy, so they are useless in the moment. They suit predictable seasonal allergy in patients who cannot tolerate other therapy. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which glaucoma agent is CONTRAINDICATED in children under two years of age?","choices":["Latanoprost","Brimonidine","Timolol","Dorzolamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The alpha-2 agonists, because of central nervous system depression and apnea. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which dihydropyridine is available INTRAVENOUSLY?","choices":["Amlodipine","Felodipine","Nicardipine","Nimodipine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dihydropyridines are oral only, except nicardipine. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which drug's concentrations are LOWERED BY ANTACIDS?","choices":["Rosuvastatin","Fenofibrate","Alirocumab","Ezetimibe"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Antacids and bile acid sequestrants decrease ezetimibe concentrations; cyclosporine increases them. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic class inhibits DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV, breaking double-stranded DNA?","choices":["Moxifloxacin","Erythromycin","Gentamicin","Natamycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The fluoroquinolone mechanism: supercoiled DNA cannot relax, and the strand breaks. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which class inhibits mast cell degranulation, limiting release of histamine, tryptase and prostaglandin D2?","choices":["Alcaftadine","Nedocromil","Bromfenac","Loteprednol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The mast cell stabilizers, which also dampen mediator release from basophils, eosinophils and neutrophils. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which alpha-2 agonist is MORE LIPOPHILIC?","choices":["Apraclonidine","Timolol","Brimonidine","Pilocarpine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Apraclonidine is highly ionized at physiological pH; brimonidine is the more lipophilic of the two, and allergic conjunctivitis is less common with it. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker raises DIGOXIN levels by inhibiting P-glycoprotein?","choices":["Amlodipine","Nifedipine","Felodipine","Verapamil"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Non-dihydropyridines inhibit P-glycoprotein (raising digoxin) and CYP3A4 (raising three statins). — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which drug ACTIVATES PPAR-ALPHA to lower triglycerides and raise HDL (high-density lipoprotein)?","choices":["Fenofibrate","Ezetimibe","Colestipol","Atorvastatin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fibrates activate PPAR-alpha: more fatty acid oxidation, less VLDL (very low-density lipoprotein), more ApoA-1 and HDL (high-density lipoprotein). — Lipids.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic agent antagonizes PABA to block folic acid synthesis?","choices":["Trimethoprim","Sulfacetamide","Polymyxin B","Bacitracin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Interfering with folate synthesis stops bacterial growth. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How long should a patient be given to judge the FULL effect of an ophthalmic antihistamine?","choices":["Two days","Six weeks","Two weeks","One day"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Onset is within minutes, but full efficacy takes about two weeks to assess. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which class reduces aqueous production by inhibiting carbonic anhydrase in the ciliary body epithelium?","choices":["Bimatoprost","Betaxolol","Carbachol","Dorzolamide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Less bicarbonate, less fluid transport, lower pressure. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker RAISES SIMVASTATIN levels by inhibiting CYP3A4?","choices":["Diltiazem","Amlodipine","Nifedipine","Felodipine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Non-dihydropyridines inhibit CYP3A4, raising atorvastatin, lovastatin and simvastatin. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which drug INCREASES the ANTICOAGULANT EFFECT of warfarin?","choices":["Colesevelam","Gemfibrozil","Ezetimibe","Evolocumab"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fibrates increase the anticoagulant effect of warfarin. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which agent binds phospholipids on the bacterial cytoplasmic membrane and lets intracellular contents leak out?","choices":["Trimethoprim","Bacitracin","Polymyxin B","Erythromycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A membrane-permeability agent, partnered with trimethoprim in the combination drop. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic class blocks cyclooxygenase, stopping conversion of arachidonic acid to prostaglandins and thromboxanes?","choices":["Prednisolone","Cyclosporine","Cromolyn","Ketorolac"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The ophthalmic non-steroidals. The mechanism is the same whether the drug is taken for a headache or put in the eye. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which glaucoma class causes a BITTER TASTE in about a quarter of patients and stinging in about a third?","choices":["Brinzolamide","Latanoprost","Timolol","Brimonidine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, which also cause allergic conjunctivitis. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which drug is most likely to cause REFLEX TACHYCARDIA?","choices":["Verapamil","Nifedipine","Diltiazem","Metoprolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dihydropyridines dilate arteries strongly and provoke rebound tachycardia; immediate-release forms are avoided in unstable angina. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which fibrate is named as a RELATIVE CONTRAINDICATION with statins?","choices":["Fenofibrate","Bezafibrate","Gemfibrozil","Ezetimibe"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Gemfibrozil sits on the statin relative contraindication list with cyclosporine, niacin and erythromycin, because of myopathy. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic class is PREFERRED for corneal ulcers or suspected Pseudomonas aeruginosa?","choices":["Erythromycin","Bacitracin","Sulfacetamide","Moxifloxacin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The fluoroquinolones are the reach for ulcers and for the gram-negative rod, though they are expensive and resistance is emerging. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What are the ophthalmic non-steroidals indicated for?","choices":["Bromfenac — postoperative inflammation and pain, and allergic conjunctivitis","Natamycin — fungal keratitis","Timolol — open-angle glaucoma","Trifluridine — herpes simplex keratitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. And they are NOT routinely recommended for conjunctivitis generally. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which class activates muscarinic receptors, contracting the ciliary muscle to facilitate outflow?","choices":["Timolol","Pilocarpine","Brimonidine","Dorzolamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The cholinergic agonists. Compliance is poor because of side effects and frequent administration. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is beta-1 selective and given INTRAVENOUSLY?","choices":["Atenolol","Bisoprolol","Esmolol","Acebutolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Esmolol is the second-generation, beta-1 selective agent marked for intravenous use. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which drug is a BILE ACID SEQUESTRANT?","choices":["Ezetimibe","Fenofibrate","Pravastatin","Cholestyramine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The bile acid sequestrants are cholestyramine, colestipol and colesevelam. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A contact lens wearer has conjunctivitis and keratitis has been excluded. Which antibiotic class is preferred, and why?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin","Erythromycin","Bacitracin","Azithromycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fluoroquinolones, because contact lens wearers are at high risk for Pseudomonas. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic class can RAISE intraocular pressure as an adverse effect, alongside lacrimation and keratitis?","choices":["Cromolyn","Nepafenac","Ketotifen","Natamycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The ophthalmic non-steroidals. Glucocorticoids raise pressure too, but the non-steroidal list is lacrimation, keratitis, raised pressure and irritation. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which glaucoma class causes fixed, small pupils, myopia and visual disturbance — and is especially poorly tolerated by YOUNGER patients?","choices":["Latanoprost","Brinzolamide","Carbachol","Betaxolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The cholinergic agonists. Younger patients are usually intolerant of miotic therapy because of the visual blurring. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which beta blocker's HIGH LIPID SOLUBILITY brings nightmares and depression?","choices":["Atenolol","Nadolol","Sotalol","Propranolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Highly lipid-soluble agents cause more central effects: depression, nightmares, vivid dreams and hallucinations. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which drug is CONTRAINDICATED when TRIGLYCERIDES exceed 400 mg/dL?","choices":["Colestipol","Fenofibrate","Gemfibrozil","Niacin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Resins may raise VLDL (very low-density lipoprotein) and triglycerides; above 400 mg/dL is an absolute contraindication. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic class carries a risk of corneal ulceration and reactive keratoconjunctivitis after several days of use?","choices":["Erythromycin","Tobramycin","Bacitracin","Azithromycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The aminoglycoside-specific risk, and one of the few adverse effects worth attaching to a single class. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which class suppresses the LATE phase allergic reaction by inhibiting phospholipase A2?","choices":["Ketorolac","Olopatadine","Prednisolone","Lodoxamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The glucocorticoids, cutting off arachidonic acid derived mediators at source. They also inhibit fibrin and collagen deposition, reducing scar formation. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which cholinergic agonist is used in the SURGICAL setting?","choices":["Pilocarpine","Carbachol","Tropicamide","Acetylcholine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The surgical agent among the three cholinergic agonists. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which beta blocker ALSO BLOCKS ALPHA-1 and is used in heart failure?","choices":["Carvedilol","Metoprolol","Atenolol","Nadolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Carvedilol is a third-generation agent with alpha-1 blockade, antioxidant and antiproliferative actions; it is a heart failure agent. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which lipid-lowering drug is APPROVED IN PREGNANCY because it is not absorbed?","choices":["Atorvastatin","Cholestyramine","Fenofibrate","Simvastatin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bile acid sequestrants are not absorbed, so they are approved for children, adolescents and pregnancy. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic class is associated with an unpleasant taste after instillation?","choices":["Bacitracin","Erythromycin","Levofloxacin","Gentamicin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A fluoroquinolone effect, and it reflects nasolacrimal drainage carrying drug to the throat. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which glucocorticoid is a SOFT steroid with a lower risk of raising intraocular pressure?","choices":["Dexamethasone","Prednisolone","Difluprednate","Loteprednol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. One of the three soft steroids, with fluorometholone and rimexolone. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A patient's pressure is not controlled on one agent. What is the principle behind adding a second?","choices":["Choose a DIFFERENT mechanism, for synergy and fewer drops","Choose an agent of the same class at a higher strength","Add a topical glucocorticoid","Add a topical vasoconstrictor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Targeting multiple routes lowers pressure synergistically, and fewer drops improves compliance. Adding a drug of the same category would be the wrong move. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which beta blocker blocks alpha-1 AND acts as a BETA-2 AGONIST?","choices":["Propranolol","Labetalol","Bisoprolol","Esmolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Labetalol is a third-generation agent with alpha-1 blockade and beta-2 agonist activity. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which drug INTERFERES WITH ABSORPTION of digoxin, warfarin and thyroxine?","choices":["Evolocumab","Fenofibrate","Colesevelam","Rosuvastatin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Anion-exchange resins bind these drugs in the gut; give them 1 hour before or 4 hours after the resin. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which is the ONLY commercially available ophthalmic antifungal?","choices":["Amphotericin B","Fluconazole","Miconazole","Natamycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Everything else has to be compounded by a specialty pharmacy or given systemically. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which ocular glucocorticoid is given by INTRAVITREAL injection?","choices":["Triamcinolone","Fluorometholone","Rimexolone","Loteprednol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The intravitreal agent among the ophthalmic steroids. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What is the general goal of glaucoma therapy, in terms of pressure?","choices":["A 50 per cent reduction","A 20 to 30 per cent reduction","Any reduction at all","Normalizing to below 10 mmHg"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. And therapy can be started in one eye to judge efficacy and tolerability. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is one of the three used for HEART FAILURE?","choices":["Propranolol","Atenolol","Bisoprolol","Timolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The heart failure beta blockers are carvedilol, metoprolol succinate and bisoprolol, started very low and increased slowly. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which drug causes PROSTAGLANDIN-MEDIATED FLUSHING, minimized by aspirin?","choices":["Fenofibrate","Ezetimibe","Colestipol","Niacin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Niacin flushing is prostaglandin mediated; aspirin premedication minimizes it. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which antifungal increases fungal cell membrane permeability by binding sterol?","choices":["Natamycin","Fluconazole","Trifluridine","Ganciclovir"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It covers Aspergillus, Candida, Cephalosporium, Fusarium and Penicillium. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Ocular glucocorticoids are limited to a pulse of what length?","choices":["Less than six weeks","Less than two weeks","Less than 72 hours","No limit"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Their risks accumulate: cataract, raised pressure and glaucoma, infection from reduced immune function, delayed wound healing, and corneal ulcers. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which patients with ocular hypertension are treated rather than monitored?","choices":["All patients with raised pressure","Only those already blind in one eye","Those WITH risk factors","Only those under 40"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Those without risk factors are monitored until glaucomatous changes occur. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is used for GLAUCOMA, reducing aqueous humor production?","choices":["Esmolol","Atenolol","Labetalol","Timolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Timolol reduces aqueous humor production in the ciliary body; systemic effects of the drops are notable. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which drug RAISES URIC ACID and glucose, so gout and diabetes are relative contraindications?","choices":["Niacin","Gemfibrozil","Ezetimibe","Cholestyramine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. At larger doses niacin raises liver enzymes, glucose and uric acid; chronic liver disease is its absolute contraindication. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which topical antiviral interferes with viral replication by inhibiting thymidylate synthetase and taking the place of thymidine in viral DNA?","choices":["Ganciclovir","Trifluridine","Acyclovir","Foscarnet"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Used for herpes simplex keratoconjunctivitis. Its ocular toxicity is punctate keratopathy. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which patients are at greater risk of raised intraocular pressure from ocular steroids?","choices":["Contact lens wearers","Children under two","Those with a FAMILY HISTORY","Patients with asthma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Elevated pressure is more likely with a family history, alongside the other steroid risks. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which ocular anesthetic leaves the eye numb for 10 to 20 minutes with NO BLINK REFLEX?","choices":["Tropicamide","Phenylephrine","Fluorescein","Proparacaine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. True of both ocular anesthetics, and the reason you must not write prescriptions for them. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is used for MIGRAINE PROPHYLAXIS?","choices":["Propranolol","Esmolol","Bisoprolol","Atenolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Propranolol (with timolol) is used for migraine prophylaxis at low dose; the mechanism is uncertain. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which form of niacin is NOT EFFECTIVE as an antilipemic?","choices":["Nicotinic acid","Niacinamide","Extended-release niacin","Immediate-release niacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nicotinic acid is the antilipemic form; its amide, niacinamide (nicotinamide), is not effective. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which antiviral competitively inhibits the binding of deoxyguanosine triphosphate to DNA polymerase?","choices":["Trifluridine","Valacyclovir","Ganciclovir","Cidofovir"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Used for herpetic keratitis, and for cytomegalovirus retinitis by intravitreal injection. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which drug inhibits production and release of interleukin 2, reducing T cell activation, and is used for chronic dry eye?","choices":["Prednisolone","Ketorolac","Lodoxamide","Cyclosporine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It reduces inflammatory markers in the lacrimal gland and increases tear production. Its commonest adverse effect is ocular burning. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which ocular agents should you NOT write prescriptions for?","choices":["Tetracaine","Latanoprost","Olopatadine","Erythromycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The ocular anesthetics. Used in clinic for tonometry, foreign body removal and superficial corneal surgery, but not sent home. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is NON-SELECTIVE (first generation)?","choices":["Metoprolol","Nadolol","Atenolol","Bisoprolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The first-generation non-selective agents are nadolol, penbutolol, pindolol, propranolol, sotalol and timolol. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which drug produces the LARGEST RISE IN HDL (high-density lipoprotein)?","choices":["Ezetimibe","Colesevelam","Niacin","Pravastatin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nicotinic acid raises HDL by 15 to 35%, the largest rise of any class. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which antiviral is given INTRAVENOUSLY for cytomegalovirus retinitis?","choices":["Trifluridine","Famciclovir","Natamycin","Foscarnet"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cytomegalovirus retinitis is the intravenous indication, alongside ganciclovir and cidofovir. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Cyclosporine eye drops are indicated for which condition?","choices":["Keratoconjunctivitis sicca","Bacterial conjunctivitis","Open-angle glaucoma","Herpes simplex keratitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chronic dry eye associated with inflammation. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which class competitively blocks muscarinic receptors to produce mydriasis for a fundoscopic examination?","choices":["Phenylephrine","Tropicamide","Pilocarpine","Proparacaine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The antimuscarinic cycloplegics, with atropine and cyclopentolate. Also used in uveitis to prevent synechiae and relieve ciliary spasm. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which alpha-1 blocker is ALPHA-1A SELECTIVE, treating the prostate with little vascular effect?","choices":["Prazosin","Terazosin","Tamsulosin","Doxazosin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tamsulosin selectively blocks alpha-1A receptors; vascular receptors are alpha-1B, so blood pressure effects are limited. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Which drug causes CHOLELITHIASIS and is contraindicated in existing gallbladder disease?","choices":["Niacin","Colestipol","Rosuvastatin","Fenofibrate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fibrates cause cholelithiasis and are contraindicated in existing gallbladder disease. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Adenoviral conjunctivitis is diagnosed. Which antiviral is indicated?","choices":["None — there is no antiviral for adenoviral conjunctivitis","Trifluridine","Ganciclovir","Valacyclovir"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It runs a self-limited course and is treated with symptomatic relief of irritation. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which systemic conditions manifest with dry eyes?","choices":["Asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Sjogren syndrome, rheumatoid arthritis, vitamin A deficiency and Stevens-Johnson syndrome","Diabetes and hypertension","Hyperthyroidism and Graves disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. And the first rule of dry eye is to treat the underlying disease. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A pupil dilated with which agent stays MORE REACTIVE TO LIGHT?","choices":["Atropine","Cyclopentolate","Phenylephrine","Tropicamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The sympathomimetic mydriatic leaves more light reactivity than the antimuscarinics do. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Which antihypertensive MILDLY LOWERS LDL (low-density lipoprotein) AND TRIGLYCERIDES and raises HDL (high-density lipoprotein)?","choices":["Propranolol","Clonidine","Hydralazine","Prazosin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prazosin shows evidence of a mild fall in LDL (low-density lipoprotein) and triglycerides with a rise in HDL (high-density lipoprotein). — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which drug is an injectable MONOCLONAL ANTIBODY against PCSK9?","choices":["Alirocumab","Ezetimibe","Colesevelam","Gemfibrozil"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Alirocumab and evolocumab are the injectable-only, expensive PCSK9 inhibitors. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which route of ocular drug delivery circumvents absorption entirely for an immediate local effect?","choices":["Topical","Intravitreal injection or device","Subconjunctival injection","Intraocular (intracameral) injection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Used for endophthalmitis, retinitis and age-related macular degeneration. Its limitation is retinal toxicity. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which principal target cells drive the ocular hypersensitivity reaction?","choices":["Neutrophils and macrophages","T cells and B cells","Mast cells and basophils","Eosinophils and dendritic cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. IgE binds their Fc receptors, and within seconds tyrosine kinases activate and histamine, platelet-activating factor and leukotrienes are released. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which agent reveals epithelial defects of the cornea and conjunctiva?","choices":["Proparacaine","Tropicamide","Phenylephrine","Fluorescein"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Used for anterior segment staining and disclosing corneal injury. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which drug is a central ALPHA-2 AGONIST that BLUNTS OPIATE WITHDRAWAL?","choices":["Clonidine","Guanfacine","Prazosin","Hydralazine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Clonidine's analgesic activity blunts opiate withdrawal reactions; it stimulates central alpha-2 and imidazoline receptors. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Which drug's MOST SERIOUS adverse reaction is HYPERSENSITIVITY?","choices":["Simvastatin","Evolocumab","Niacin","Fenofibrate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypersensitivity reactions are the most serious adverse reaction to PCSK9 inhibitors. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which ocular route risks nasal mucosal toxicity and systemic side effects through nasolacrimal absorption?","choices":["Intravitreal injection","Retrobulbar injection","Topical","Intracameral injection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Convenient, economical and relatively safe, but what drains down the nasolacrimal duct is absorbed systemically and skips first-pass metabolism. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is the normal range of intraocular pressure?","choices":["About 30 to 40 mmHg","About 2 to 8 mmHg","About 40 to 60 mmHg","About 10 to 21 mmHg"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ocular hypertension is a pressure above normal WITHOUT optic nerve damage or visual field loss. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which central agent is the MOST ALPHA-2 SELECTIVE, with LESS SEDATION?","choices":["Guanfacine","Clonidine","Prazosin","Tamsulosin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Guanfacine is less potent than clonidine, the most selective for alpha-2 over alpha-1, less sedating, with occasional withdrawal. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"Which drug and its active GLUCURONIDE metabolite circulate ENTEROHEPATICALLY?","choices":["Cholestyramine","Ezetimibe","Atorvastatin","Evolocumab"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Enterohepatic recirculation returns ezetimibe to its site of action and limits systemic exposure. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which prodrug is converted in the eye to epinephrine?","choices":["Latanoprost","Brimonidine","Dipivefrin","Timolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. One of the two ocular prodrug examples; the other is latanoprost, converted to prostaglandin F2 alpha. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Glaucoma drugs focus on which type, and why?","choices":["Closed angle, because it is more dangerous","Both equally","Neither — glaucoma is surgical","Open angle — that is where drug therapy works"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Angle-closure is an abrupt, emergent blockage of the drainage canal, usually with acute pain, and is not primarily a drug problem. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which drug RAISES BLOOD GLUCOSE by inhibiting insulin secretion and retains sodium?","choices":["Clonidine","Lisinopril","Amlodipine","Hydralazine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Clonidine increases blood glucose by inhibiting insulin secretion and causes sodium retention, so it is often given with a diuretic. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which drug is FIRST LINE when LDL (low-density lipoprotein) lowering drugs are indicated?","choices":["Ezetimibe","Atorvastatin","Colestipol","Gemfibrozil"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Statins are the most efficacious and best tolerated agents and are first line for LDL lowering. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which drug accumulates in the eye and produces a bull's eye lesion?","choices":["Latanoprost","Timolol","Chloroquine","Dorzolamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The example given of a drug distributing into and accumulating in the eye. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which two routes lower intraocular pressure, and which class does BOTH?","choices":["Latanoprost — it does both","Timolol — it does both","Dorzolamide — it does both","Brimonidine — it increases outflow AND decreases production"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Alpha adrenergic agonists appear on both lists. Prostaglandins and cholinergics increase outflow; beta blockers and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors decrease production. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which drug is N-ACETYLATED, with LUPUS SYNDROME in slow acetylators?","choices":["Hydralazine","Minoxidil","Nitroprusside","Prazosin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hydralazine lupus syndrome risk: high dose, long term, women, slow acetylators and Caucasian ancestry. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"Absorption across the cornea is governed by which law?","choices":["Poiseuille's law","Fick's law","Starling's law","Laplace's law"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Transcorneal absorption is necessary for local ocular effect, and its rate depends most on the concentration gradient. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which drug should come with the warning that STOOLS MAY TURN BLACK?","choices":["Minoxidil","Clonidine","Hydralazine","Verapamil"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stools may turn black on hydralazine; the patient is told ahead of time. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which drug OPENS ATP-MODULATED POTASSIUM CHANNELS and causes HYPERTRICHOSIS?","choices":["Hydralazine","Nitroprusside","Diltiazem","Minoxidil"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Minoxidil hyperpolarizes arteriolar smooth muscle through potassium efflux and grows hair on the face, back, arms and legs. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Which drug given by INFUSION for HYPERTENSIVE CRISIS can cause CYANIDE toxicity?","choices":["Nitroprusside","Hydralazine","Minoxidil","Nicardipine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nitroprusside carries five cyanide groups; sodium thiosulfate limits cyanide toxicity, and thiocyanate builds up with long infusions or renal failure. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which drug is CONTRAINDICATED in CORONARY ARTERY DISEASE and the ELDERLY because of reflex sympathetic workload?","choices":["Metoprolol","Hydralazine","Lisinopril","Amlodipine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hydralazine is contraindicated in coronary artery disease, the elderly and ischemia: vasodilation plus sympathetic activation raises cardiac work. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 102"}]};
